  "stemBlocks": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "The equation of a line is 4x − 2y = 10. (a) Express the equation in the form y = mx + c. (b) State the gradient. (c) Determine the x-intercept and the y-intercept."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "Think first: Rearranging to make y the subject exposes both the gradient and the y-intercept at once."
    },
    {
      "type": "asset",
      "storagePath": "assets/question_visuals/p123_q09.jpg",
      "altText": "Source visual for edmar-csec-p123-q09 from workbook page 123."
    }
  ],
  "solutionSteps": [
    {
      "stepNo": 1,
      "instruction": "Move the x term to the right-hand side.",
      "contentBlocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "4x − 2y = 10 becomes −2y = 10 − 4x"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Result: −2y = 10 − 4x"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stepNo": 2,
      "instruction": "Divide every term by −2.",
      "contentBlocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "y = 10/(−2) − 4x/(−2)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Result: y = 2x − 5"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stepNo": 3,
      "instruction": "Read the gradient from y = mx + c.",
      "contentBlocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "The coefficient of x"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Result: Gradient = 2"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stepNo": 4,
      "instruction": "Read the y-intercept from the constant term.",
      "contentBlocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "c = −5"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Result: y-intercept = −5, at (0, −5)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stepNo": 5,
      "instruction": "Set y = 0 to find the x-intercept.",
      "contentBlocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "0 = 2x − 5, so 2x = 5"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Result: x-intercept = 2.5, at (2.5, 0)"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "strategyBlocks": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "Make y the subject of 4x − 2y = 10, read m and c from the result, then set y = 0 to find the x-intercept."
    }
  ],
  "finalAnswerBlocks": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "(a) y = 2x − 5  (b) Gradient = 2  (c) x-intercept = 2.5 at (2.5, 0); y-intercept = −5 at (0, −5)"
    }
  ],
  "whyThisWorks": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "The form y = mx + c is useful precisely because m and c can be read directly, so any linear equation is worth rearranging before answering questions about slope or intercepts. Dividing by a negative number flips the sign of every term, which is where the gradient turns out positive even though the original equation shows −2y."
    }
  ],
